{"kind":"stack","id":"s1","title":"Conductors and insulators","theme":{"background":"#f7f9fc","foreground":"#1d2b3a","font":"Arial, sans-serif"}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c1","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c1-q","content":"Which material is a good conductor of electricity?"},{"type":"button","id":"c1-a","label":"Copper","why":"Copper is a metal, and metals let electricity flow through them easily.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c1-b","label":"Rubber","why":"Pupils may think rubber conducts because it feels stretchy, but it is an insulator.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c1-c","label":"Plastic","why":"Pupils may confuse plastic with metal because both are used in wires, but plastic is an insulator.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c2","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c2-t","content":"Conductor"},{"type":"text","id":"c2-e","content":"A material that lets electricity flow through it easily."},{"type":"button","id":"c2-n","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c3","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c3-q","content":"Which material is an insulator?"},{"type":"button","id":"c3-a","label":"Wood","why":"Dry wood does not let electricity flow, so it is an insulator.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-b","label":"Iron","why":"Pupils may think iron is not a metal, but iron is a metal and a conductor.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-c","label":"Silver","why":"Silver is a metal, and pupils may assume all shiny things are insulators, but silver conducts.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c4","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c4-t","content":"You are building a circuit. Do you use a metal paperclip or a plastic ruler to complete the path?"},{"type":"button","id":"c4-a","label":"Metal paperclip","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c4-b","label":"Plastic ruler","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c6"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c5","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c5-t","content":"The bulb lights up brightly. The paperclip is a conductor, so the electricity flows."},{"type":"button","id":"c5-n","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c6","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c6-t","content":"The bulb stays dark. The ruler is an insulator, so the electricity cannot flow."},{"type":"button","id":"c6-n","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c7","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c7-q","content":"Why are most metals good conductors?"},{"type":"button","id":"c7-a","label":"They let electricity flow through them easily","why":"Metals have free electrons that carry electricity through them.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c7-b","label":"They are too heavy for electricity to pass","why":"Pupils may think weight stops electricity, but weight does not decide conduction.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c7-c","label":"They stop electricity from moving","why":"Pupils may reverse the rule, but metals let electricity move through them.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c8","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c8-q","content":"Which of these is a good conductor?"},{"type":"button","id":"c8-a","label":"Aluminium foil","why":"Aluminium is a metal, so it is a good conductor.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c8-b","label":"Cotton wool","why":"Pupils may think soft things conduct, but cotton is an insulator.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c8-c","label":"Glass","why":"Pupils may think shiny things conduct, but glass is an insulator.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c9","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c9-t","content":"Insulator"},{"type":"text","id":"c9-e","content":"A material that does not let electricity flow through it easily."},{"type":"button","id":"c9-n","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c10","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c10-q","content":"Which material is an insulator used to cover wires?"},{"type":"button","id":"c10-a","label":"Plastic","why":"Plastic does not let electricity flow, so it keeps people safe.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-b","label":"Copper","why":"Pupils may think the wire itself is covered in metal, but copper is the conductor inside.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-c","label":"Steel","why":"Pupils may think steel protects wires, but steel is a metal and a conductor.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c11","kind":"end","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c11-t","content":"You finished the conductors and insulators lesson!"},{"type":"text","id":"c11-m","content":"Well done. You can now recognise good conductors and insulators."}],"actions":[]}}
